          "content": "Ganita Prakash | Grade 6"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.12,
            0.884,
            0.163
          ],
          "content": "did not land on 36, so he does not get the treasure. What jump size should he have chosen?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.193,
            0.166,
            0.882,
            0.187
          ],
          "content": "The factors of 14 are: 1, 2, 7, 14. So, these jump sizes will land on 14."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.164,
            0.182,
            0.883,
            0.232
          ],
          "content": "The factors of 36 are: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18 and 36. These jump sizes will land on 36."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.234,
            0.884,
            0.278
          ],
          "content": "So, the jump sizes of 1 or 2 will land on both 14 and 36. Notice that 1 and 2 are the common factors of 14 and 36."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.163,
            0.28,
            0.884,
            0.347
          ],
          "content": "The jump sizes using which both the treasures can be reached are the **common factors** of the two numbers where the treasures are placed."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.166,
            0.358,
            0.884,
            0.405
          ],
          "content": "What jump size can reach both 15 and 30? There are multiple jump sizes possible. Try to find them all."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.165,
            0.415,
            0.682,
            0.437
          ],
          "content": "Look at the table below. What do you notice?"
